      {
        "h_title": "The Morning Ritual: Dim Sum with a Kick",
        "main_title": "Silk Road on a Thai Plate",
        "author": "TAT Newsroom",
        "language": "en",
        "level": 3,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "One of my favorite ways to start the day in Southern Thailand, especially in places like Phuket or Trang, is by visiting a local dim sum shop. These areas have strong Hokkien roots. You’ll find bamboo steamers with shumai and har gow, but the setting feels truly Thai.",
            "url": "https://www.google.com/search?q=phuket&rlz=1C1VDKB_enTH1085TH1086&oq=Phuket&gs_lcrp=EgZjaHJvbWUqDwgAECMYJxjjAhiABBiKBTIPCAAQIxgnGOMCGIAEGIoFMgwIARAuGCcYgAQYigUyDwgCEC4YQxixAxiABBiKBTINCAMQABiRAhiABBiKBTITCAQQLhivARjHARiRAhiABBiKBTIMCAUQABhDGIAEGIoFMhIIBhAuGEMYrwEYxwEYgAQYigUyDAgHEAAYQxiABBiKBTIPCAgQABgUGIcCGLEDGIAEMgwICRAAGEMYgAQYigXSAQg0MzI4ajBqOagCBrACAfEF9JmohTHygZ4&sourceid=chrome&ie=UTF-8",
            "urls": [
              {
                "url": "https://www.google.com/search?q=phuket&rlz=1C1VDKB_enTH1085TH1086&oq=Phuket&gs_lcrp=EgZjaHJvbWUqDwgAECMYJxjjAhiABBiKBTIPCAAQIxgnGOMCGIAEGIoFMgwIARAuGCcYgAQYigUyDwgCEC4YQxixAxiABBiKBTINCAMQABiRAhiABBiKBTITCAQQLhivARjHARiRAhiABBiKBTIMCAUQABhDGIAEGIoFMhIIBhAuGEMYrwEYxwEYgAQYigUyDAgHEAAYQxiABBiKBTIPCAgQABgUGIcCGLEDGIAEMgwICRAAGEMYgAQYigXSAQg0MzI4ajBqOagCBrACAfEF9JmohTHygZ4&sourceid=chrome&ie=UTF-8",
                "anchor_text": "Phuket"
              },
              {
                "url": "https://www.tourismthailand.org/Destinations/Provinces/Trang/355",
                "anchor_text": "Trang"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "Locals enjoy their dumplings with Kopi, a strong, butter-roasted coffee with condensed milk, and often add spicy dipping sauces that might surprise someone from Guangzhou. It’s a relaxed, social way to begin the day and shows that food here is more than just a meal—it connects cultures.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
        "secondary_content": null,
        "table_content": null
      },
      {
        "h_title": "Why It Works",
        "main_title": "Silk Road on a Thai Plate",
        "author": "TAT Newsroom",
        "language": "en",
        "level": 3,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "Why does this fusion work so well? I think it’s because both cultures value the ‘perfect bite.’ The Chinese brought skills such as noodle-making, soy fermentation, and deep-frying. The Thais added bright flavors like lemongrass, lime, galangal, and those spicy chilies.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "When you sit on a plastic stool in Yaowarat and watch the charcoal flames under a big pot of Kuay Jab— rolled rice noodles in peppery broth—you’re not just having dinner. You’re tasting a story of migration, change, and shared happiness.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
